.TH SDL_FlushRenderer 3 "SDL 3.5.0" "Simple Directmedia Layer" "SDL3 FUNCTIONS"
.SH NAME
SDL_FlushRenderer \- Force the rendering context to flush any pending commands and state.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <SDL3/SDL_render.h>
.PP
.BI "bool SDL_FlushRenderer(SDL_Renderer *renderer);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
You do not need to (and in fact, shouldn\(aqt) call this function unless you are planning to call into OpenGL/Direct3D/Metal/whatever directly, in addition to using an
.BR SDL_Renderer \[char46]
.PP
This is for a very-specific case: if you are using SDL\(aqs render API, and you plan to make OpenGL/D3D/whatever calls in addition to SDL render API calls\[char46] If this applies, you should call this function between calls to SDL\(aqs render API and the low-level API you\(aqre using in cooperation\[char46]
.PP
In all other cases, you can ignore this function\[char46]
.PP
This call makes SDL flush any pending rendering work it was queueing up to do later in a single batch, and marks any internal cached state as invalid, so it\(aqll prepare all its state again later, from scratch\[char46]
.PP
This means you do not need to save state in your rendering code to protect the SDL renderer\[char46] However, there lots of arbitrary pieces of Direct3D and OpenGL state that can confuse things; you should use your best judgment and be prepared to make changes if specific state needs to be protected\[char46]

.SH FUNCTION PARAMETERS
.TP
.I renderer
the rendering context\[char46]
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns true on success or false on failure; call
.BR SDL_GetError ()
for more information\[char46]

.SH THREAD SAFETY
This function should only be called on the main thread\[char46]

.SH AVAILABILITY
This function is available since SDL 3\[char46]2\[char46]0\[char46]

